// // How to turn this micro‑service demo into a recruiter‑ready showcase  
// // 1.  Frame the story – “Why micro‑services?”
// // Recruiter claim	How you prove it (quick demo)
// // Scalable	Run two instances of a single service (e.g., BookingService) behind a load‑balancer (Docker‑Compose + NGINX) and show CPU / request‑per‑second metrics climb linearly.
// // Fast	Cache expensive read‑paths (hotel search, availability) in Redis and expose latency graphs (Grafana) that drop from ≈ 200 ms → ≈ 30 ms.
// // Resilient	Add a circuit‑breaker (e.g., github.com/sony/gobreaker) around each downstream call and demonstrate a failing service being bypassed without taking the whole API down.
// // Polyglot	Keep the existing Go auth & review services and add a tiny Node/Express “notifications” service that publishes to a RabbitMQ queue – show two different runtimes co‑operating.
// // When you talk, point to a single “demo dashboard” (Grafana + Prometheus) that visualises all the above.  Recruiters love a live screenshot or a short screen‑recording that shows metrics changing as you scale the containers.
// // 2.  Immediate‑win checklist (high‑impact, low‑effort)
// // Area	Quick win
// // Observability	• Add Prometheus client (promhttp) to every service.<br>• Export request count, latency, error rate.<br>• Spin up Grafana dashboard.
// // Search performance	• Deploy an Elasticsearch node (docker‑compose).<br>• Index hotels on name, location, amenities.<br>• Add a thin Go client in HotelService that queries ES first, falls back to DB.<br>• Cache the ES response in Redis (TTL ≈ 5 min).
// // User experience	• Add a /profile endpoint (GET/PUT) that stores extra fields (avatar URL, phone).<br>• Add a “wishlist” table + API (/users/{id}/wishlist).
// // RBAC & auth hardening	• Turn RequireAllRoles / RequireAnyRole into a reusable middleware that reads roles from the DB via DI (inject repository).<br>• Add a role claim to JWTs and protect a new /admin/* route.
// Hotel data enrichment	• Extend hotel schema: amenities []string, image_urls []string, contact {phone,email}.<br>• Add migration + API fields (POST /hotels, GET /hotels/:id).
// Geospatial indexing	• Switch the latitude/longitude columns to a PostGIS POINT type.<br>• Expose /hotels/near?lat=&lng=&radius= endpoint that uses ST_DWithin.
// Payment flow (mock)	• Add /payments/create‑order, /payments/verify, /payments/webhook endpoints that call a fake Stripe/PayPal mock service.<br>• Store order_id, status in DB.
// Containerisation	• Write a multi‑stage Dockerfile for each Go service (compile → slim runtime).<br>• Add a docker‑compose.yml that brings up all services, Redis, Elasticsearch, PostgreSQL, and a reverse‑proxy (NGINX).
// OWASP hardening	• Replace any remaining fmt.Sprintf SQL with prepared statements (most already use ?).<br>• DI: inject the UserRoleRepository into the RequireAllRoles middleware instead of creating it inside the handler.<br>• Remove the global logger that prints request/response bodies; keep only request IDs and status codes.<br>• Change DB DSN to sslmode=require (or verify-full for prod).<br>• In utils/auth.go set cookies HttpOnly: true, Secure: true, SameSite: Strict.<br>• Add a rate‑limit wrapper around the login endpoint (RateLimitMiddleware(5) ≈ 5 req/min).<br>• Implement account‑lockout after 5 failed logins and a password‑reset token flow (email‑token stored with TTL).<br>• Add a security‑headers middleware (Helmet) that sets CSP, X‑Content‑Type‑Options, X‑Frame‑Options, Referrer‑Policy, HSTS.
// Documentation / README	• Add a one‑page “Architecture diagram” (draw.io or Mermaid) with service boundaries, data flow, and the new observability stack.<br>• List “Run locally” steps (Docker‑Compose).<br>• Highlight the “Immediate wins” you just implemented.
// 3.  Prioritisation for a fresh‑grad interview demo
// 1. Observability + Docker – First 24 h. A working docker compose up plus a Grafana screenshot instantly validates “I can ship & run.”  
// 2. Search (Elasticsearch + Redis) – Next 2 days. Shows you can wire up a “real‑world” search engine and caching layer.  
// 3. User profile & wishlist – 1 day. Gives you UI‑ready data for a portfolio front‑end (React or simple HTML).  
// 4. Security hardening – 2 days. Replace default JWT secrets, secure cookies, add rate limiting & lockout.  
// 5. Hotel data enrichment & geospatial query – 2 days. Adds depth to the domain model and a cool “find hotels nearby” demo.  
// 6. Payment mock – 1‑2 days if time permits; otherwise keep as a stub with comments.
// Tip: Show, don’t just tell. Record a 60‑second video that starts the stack, runs a load‑test (hey -z 10s -c 20 http://localhost:3000/api/v1/hotels?city=Paris), then flips to the Grafana dashboard showing latency dropping after the Redis cache warms up.
// 4.  Talking points to counter the “just label‑as‑scalable” objection
// - Metrics‑first – “I never claim scalability without evidence. Here’s a live Prometheus graph that proves the API handles 500 RPS with < 40 ms latency when two instances run behind NGINX.”
// - Isolation – “Each service has its own DB schema and can be independently versioned; adding a new language (Node) or a new domain (reviews) does not affect the existing code‑base.”
// - Resilience – “Circuit‑breaker + retry policies prevent a single downstream failure from cascading. I can demonstrate the system staying up even when the Payment mock goes down.”
// - Cost‑effective scaling – “Because we cache search results in Redis, we reduce DB load by ~80 % (observed from the Grafana query‑rate chart). Less DB traffic → cheaper cloud‑instance scaling.”
// - Future‑ready – “The architecture already supports Kubernetes (deployment yaml in k8s/ folder). All services expose health checks (/healthz) for auto‑restart by an orchestrator.”
